\section{The two wins}{

\code{.calcFactorTMM} builds its trim mask from \code{rank(logR)} and \code{rank(absE)} twice each,
which is four sorts where two would do. \code{rank} is rebound to compute each vector once,
which is bit-identical and needs no workers at all: measured 1.28s to 0.81s on
20000 x 200, \code{identical()} TRUE, max abs diff 0. On top of that the original's per-column
loops are dispatched across workers, taking the same call to 0.29s at four workers.
}

\section{Why the axis is columns}{

Every method here ranks, medians or takes a quantile across genes, so a gene-row block is
not a smaller version of the problem. The per-sample factor is independent only once the
pooled quantities are fixed, which is why the original is called once on the full matrix
rather than once per block. Splitting the matrix would also have to rebuild
\code{lib.size <- colSums(x)}, and \code{colSums} over fractional doubles is not block-associative
on arm64, where \code{sizeof.longdouble} is 8: about 1.8e-14 relative, on the quantity every
method divides by.
}

\section{Dispatches too small to be worth a fork}{

A fork round trip costs about 6 to 12 ms on an M6, so small matrices are slower
parallelized than not, and each path has its own threshold because they do not cost the same
per cell. The TMM and TMMwsp loops dispatch at or above
\code{getOption("combat.min.norm.cells", 2e5)} cells: TMM measured on an M6 with the gate forced
open at 0.72x to 1.06x at 1e5, 1.20x to 1.58x at 2e5 and 2.07x to 2.47x at 5e5, over two runs.
The RLE and upperquartile loops take one order statistic per column and cost roughly a
fifteenth as much, so they wait for \code{getOption("combat.min.order.cells", 4e6)}: RLE measured
1.31x to 1.61x at 4e6 and 1.65x to 1.97x at 1e7. The
\code{rank} hoist is not gated, because it never costs anything, and neither is \code{workers = 1}.

Those defaults and measurements apply to a backend whose workers inherit the matrix: a
fork (\code{mclapply}, a forking \code{doParallel} or \code{doMC} registration, a multicore \code{future}
plan), BiocParallel, or a function backend. On a backend that copies the matrix to each
worker (\code{serial}, \code{future} without a multicore plan, \code{foreach} with its own cluster,
